.TH STADIUM 1
.SH NAME
stadium \- stadium ball-throw toy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B temple/stadium
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Stadium
is a stadium ball-throw toy.
It runs under
.IR wm (1).
.PP
Button 1 throws a ball among wandering fans. Enter reseeds the crowd.
.PP
The fans are one vector sprite,
.BR /lib/temple/sprites/stadium_1.spr ,
drawn through
.IR vsprite (2)
at each fan's wander angle.
A wandering fan's angle only ever moves by a twenty-fifth of a radian and
is put back to zero outside
.BR \(+-0.75 ,
so there are thirty-nine of them and each raster is made once and kept.
A fan the ball knocks over is the exception: it is frozen at
.BR -\(*p/2 ,
outside that range and never tested against it again, so the cache
reaches further than the wander does, and an angle outside even that is
drawn without the cache rather than snapped to the nearest slot.
.PP
The controls are:
.TF space
.TP
.B LMB
Throw ball toward click
.TP
.B Enter
Restart
.TP
.B Esc / q
Quit
.PD
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/temple/stadium.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR temple (1),
.IR vsprite (2),
.IR draw3d (2),
.IR wm (1)
.SH BUGS
The fan changed when it moved onto
.IR vsprite (2).
The port used to walk the element list itself and draw each line through
.IR draw (2),
which is a different rasteriser from the one
.B scripts/tos_extract_sprites.py
used, and about fourteen of a hundred and eight inked pixels came out
elsewhere \(em twenty-two of them at angle zero, where the sprite's own
.B .bit
says the new render is the right one and the old was not.
So this is not the same picture it was, on purpose.
.PP
The cache nearly made it a worse one.
Sized to the wander alone and indexed by a clamped key, it drew the
knocked-over fan at
.B -0.76
radians instead of
.BR -\(*p/2 ,
so a fan flattened by the ball stood almost upright: 202 pixels different
where every other angle differs by about 14.
Neither the sprite comparison nor
.IR temple (1)'s
smoke test could have seen it \(em one never asks for that angle and the
other never throws the ball.
